    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This paper is a progress report on research concerning the evolution of strike-slip networks in sandstone. In the Valley-of-Fire State Park of southern Nevada, the Juras- sic Aztec sandstone is deformed by two strike-slip fault sets with different orientations and opposite slip sense. One fault set is oriented north-northeasterly and shows apparent left-lateral displacements up to 2.4 km. The other fault set is oriented northwesterly and shows apparent right-lateral offsets up to 50 m. At a regional scale, most of the right-lateral faults terminate against the larger- offset left-lateral faults and are found localized both be- tween step regions along individual left-lateral faults, as well as at the ends of the larger left-lateral faults. At a smaller scale, right- and left-lateral faults show mutu- ally abutting relationships. Also, mode I splay fractures related to fault slip are observed sharing the same ori- entation and abutting relationships as both fault sets. A working model for the evolution of the strike-slip fault network in the Valley-of-Fire is presented whereby the fault network forms via progressive splay fracturing be- tween fault segments and later shearing of the splay frac- tures. At the scale of observation made in this study, at least five generations of structures are identified.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
